Z||000000||geyser||12-21-1999||02:08 AM||||I am going to add a rear swaybar to my 87 GT. there are a few to choose from, what kind of experiences has anyone had? What brands? What sizes?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||12-21-1999||06:53 AM||||I bought the Addco from JC Whitney and love it.<BR>This is probably the most cost effective upgrade you can make to your suspension.<P>It is item number 55ND0892U and cost $99.98 with the poly bushings.  Be sure and order it with the poly bushings.  It's cheaper than ordering the poly bushings separately.<P>Good luck and have a great Christmas,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000002||geyser||12-22-1999||03:06 AM||||Thanks for the info Carrol, I will probably go with JC Whitney.||||reg||1
Z||000003||william_thorniii||12-22-1999||05:44 PM||||Do either of you know the diameter & thickness of this swaybar?  Or if you have it installed, does it not create an "oversteer" condition?  Usually adding a swaybar decreases the handling characteristics of a car.  The Fiero was designed specifically to "understeer".  It is general concensus amoung automotive handling specialists that an understeer condition is much safer and easier to correct than is an oversteer condition.  I am not telling you to do this, but I think that you should be careful.  I personally plan on moving a front sway bar to the rear and purchasing another sway bar (with increased stiffness) for the front.  Unfortunately, I am just beginning my research and have not found any likely donor cars as of yet.<P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||12-22-1999||05:47 PM||||This is from memory(and my memory is pretty foggy right now). I believe the stock front and rear sway bars on the '88s is 1" front, and 7/8" rear. I could be wrong, it's happened before  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>For those of you who have, or have seen the Motorweek video, the '86, and '87 GT, was supposed to have terrible trailing throttle oversteer. They show the car doing an emergency manuver(sp?) avoiding a pylon, while lifting off the gas, the GT pulls a 360. When they stay on the gas, the GT is still very twitchy but does not spin out. I doubt you would encounter this stuff in normal driving though.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-22-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||12-22-1999||07:25 PM||||Unless "normal driving" includes emergency maneuvers... like perhaps trying to avoid a pylon...<P><BR>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||12-22-1999||10:35 PM||||What if the pylon was shaped more like a piece of wood and you were trying to avoid it?  Or a dog?  Or...?||||reg||1
Z||000007||geyser||12-22-1999||11:08 PM||||William, the sway bars offered by JC whitney are: front 1", rear 7/8". As you are aware,the effects of sway bars are related to suspension type. For the Fiero the addition of the front bar assists the understeer condition. The addition of a rear bar could in theory cancel some of this understeer and possibly be dangerous at high highway speeds. I haven't tried one yet but I'm researching the issue. Actually I'm more interested in doing something about the Fiero "bump steer", any suggestions? 87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tim||12-22-1999||11:46 PM||||I've been running a JC Whitney rear bar about five years on my GT.  BIG DIFFERENCE!!  Without the bar, had too be careful in the turns, with the bar just push harder.  Get the poly, rubber will mush out in a year.  Don't worry about the "balance", Fieros need a rear bar.  To stiffen the front, just add poly.  Do the lube mod to the poly before you install, they will hear it.  Not bad, you just know it is not right.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tim||12-23-1999||03:55 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com/stam-bar.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com/stam-bar.htm</A> <BR>More info||||reg||1
