Z||000011||fiero56||12-23-1999||05:06 PM||||Ok guys,<BR>you got me. What I meant was, having to pull an emergency manuver every day. Plus they weren't just throwing it around, they were really cranking on the wheel, burying the corner of the nose almost into the pavement. It might just be me, but I've only had to avoid potholes in the road. Umm, maybe a dead racoon once or twice.||||reg||1
Z||000012||geyser||12-23-1999||10:50 PM||||Tim, my 87 GT tends to "bump steer" at even the smallest road imperfections while in a turn; I just purchased the car and don't know if the previous owner had done any work to the suspension. There is no rear sway bar. Sounds like what you did to yours may be the answer for me.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Tim||12-23-1999||11:55 PM||||geyser<BR>Yes, the car feels better with the bar and holds better.  I remember that before the bar I hit a curve at about 60mph.  The car broke loose and I came out of the curve backwards.  I and the guy with me knew we were lucky.  I put the bar on a couple weeks later. The same guy and I tried that curve again at 60, no problem.  I was wanting to try 90, but we settled for 80.  Why push it?  Your car will handle better.  This summer I added poly bushings.  Start with the poly.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||RFMike||12-24-1999||12:27 PM||||OK, this looks like a good time to look at Fiero handling, especially with slippery conditions in the northern part of the country.<BR>One: the Fiero will typically understeer.  This means that the front end will slide in a corner before the rear breaks loose.  You can stop the slide by gently easing off the throttle.  If you allow the throttle to snap closed, and you are in a slide (or emergency maneuver), you will have created oversteer.  This is obviously the opposite of understeer, with the rear breaking loose while the front has better adhesion.  That's why the test drivers did a 360 in the video.   Slamming the brakes on accentuates the problem.<BR>We may not have to avoid a pylon at highway speed, but anybody who wants to safely drive in winter should get out and explore what this car will do.  Remember, when the front starts to slide, ease off the throttle.  If the rear starts to slide, steer into the slide, and feather the peddle.  It is fairly easy to master, just do it in a parking lot, or a frozen lake.<BR>Back to the sway bar debate:  increasing the front sway bar dia will increase understeer, in a car that already understeers.  Increasing the rear sway bar dia will increase oversteer (bring it closer to neutral).  It will only be unsafe if you haven't mastered over and understeer corrections.  You can have either if these conditions if you incorrectly apply throttle, brake, or steering input.<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cooter||12-24-1999||12:36 PM||||I added the Fiero Store's rear bar to my 85 se (4cyl) and I really can't tell that it made much difference. I guess I was expecting a big change and it did not happen. The only thing that I found is that under hard cornering or wet conditions, the rear does tend to want to get loose, but it is very predictable and controllable. After buying my 88GT, I can already tell in the short time that I have had it that the handling is MUCH better than the 85, even though the 85 has better tires, new shocks and struts.||||reg||1
